# REQ-001: Unified Inbound Document Inbox

**Priority:** Must Have
**Effort:** High
**Risk:** High
**Depends On:** None
**Increment:** 1 — Inbox & Triage
**Outcome:** I can receive, view, and organize documents in one place

> **Increment 1 scope boundary:** This file covers the unified inbox with two tabs (Unmatched Documents and Patient Matched Documents), triage, manual patient matching, document viewing, and queue navigation. Won't be filed, Reject, the Filing Queue workflow, coding, and task creation are in later increments and are captured in the dedicated deferred sections below.

---

## User Story

As a practice administrator,
I need to manage all inbound documents from a single, unified starting point,
So that I can quickly triage and begin processing documents safely and efficiently without context-switching.

---

## ✅ Flow Variant Decision — **Confirmed: Approach B Adopted** (2026-05-26)

**Decision:** Approach B is the confirmed entry-point design. Approach A is rejected. All acceptance criteria, PxD, and evaluation checks apply Approach B only.

### ~~Approach A — Separate Inbound Inbox~~ *(Rejected)*

~~All inbound documents land in a dedicated **Inbox** view. Users triage from the Inbox, then explicitly progress each document to the Filing Queue as a separate step.~~

- ~~**Entry point:** Inbox~~
- ~~**Workflow:** Inbox → Filing Queue → Coding Queue → Tasking → Complete~~
- **Rejection reason:** requires an explicit move step from Inbox to Filing Queue — unnecessary friction at high volume; Filing Queue itself serves as triage view.
- **Prototype (reference only):** `ui-samples/approach-a-inbox-then-filing.html`

### Approach B — Unified Inbox with 2 Tabs *(Adopted — Increment 1)*

The Documents Manager inbox presents **two tabs** in Increment 1: **Unmatched Documents** (default, leftmost) and **Patient Matched Documents**. All inbound documents land in one of these two tabs based on automatic patient matching status. Users resolve unmatched documents via manual patient search, after which the document moves to the Patient Matched Documents tab. Filing, coding, tasking, and rejection actions are deferred to later increments.

- **Entry point:** Unmatched Documents tab (default active)
- **Workflow (Increment 1):** Unmatched Documents → manual patient match → Patient Matched Documents
- **Unmatched Documents tab:** Default landing tab; user resolves patient match before the document moves to the matched queue
- **Patient Matched Documents tab:** Shows all documents with a confirmed patient match, awaiting filing (Increment 2)
- **Prototype:** `ui-samples/REQ-001-prototype.html` (v3)

---

**Acceptance Criteria — Both Approaches:**
- [ ] Each document row displays all minimum triage metadata fields in the following column order: Urgency indicator, Document Title, Date Received, Matched Patient (patient name; shown when a patient match exists; blank when unmatched), Document Type, Source, and Department.
- [ ] Urgent documents are indicated by a red arrow displayed to the left of the document row. Non-urgent documents display no arrow. This is a binary indicator only — urgency categorisation (Urgent / Routine / Administrative) is a concept belonging to the Task Queue, not the document inbox. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Moving the mouse over a document row highlights that row visually to indicate hover state. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Users can triage by urgency and metadata without opening the document.
- [ ] The document list remains usable at high volumes and during backlogs.
- [ ] System autosaves state so users can resume work after interruptions without data loss.
- [ ] After patient match is confirmed on a document, the system automatically advances to the next document in the queue without returning to the queue list, preserving the current sort and filter order. *(Must Have — Increment 1: required step is patient match confirmation only)*
- [ ] The user can return to the queue document list at any point during processing using a "Back to queue" action; the current document's in-progress state is autosaved and the document is visibly marked as in-progress in the queue list.
- [ ] When the queue is exhausted after completing a document, the system returns the user to the queue list with an appropriate empty-state message.
- [ ] When navigating to the next document, the previous document's patient context is fully cleared before the new document's patient data is presented.
- [ ] The actions menu trigger (⋮) is visually hidden at rest and becomes visible when the row is hovered or receives keyboard focus. *(V1d — interaction)*
- [ ] Queue tabs use manual keyboard activation — arrow keys move focus between tabs; Enter or Space activates the focused tab and triggers a data fetch. *(V1d — accessibility)*
- [ ] On viewports narrower than 768px, the queue document list renders as a card-per-document list; the urgency indicator, document title, date received, and matched patient name are visible on each card. *(V1d — responsive)*

**Acceptance Criteria — Unmatched Tab (Approach B):**
- [ ] A dedicated **Unmatched** tab is present within the Filing Queue view, showing all documents where no patient match could be automatically determined. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] A document appears in the Unmatched tab when: (a) no valid NHS Number could be extracted, (b) the extracted NHS Number does not match any patient in the EMIS Web register, or (c) multiple candidate patients were found. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] From the Unmatched tab, the user can open a document and perform a manual patient search. The patient search queries the **EMIS Web patient register** as the authoritative data source; results are drawn live from EMIS Web and not from a local cache or separate index. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] The manual patient search UI presents results returned from EMIS Web, allowing the user to select the correct patient record. The selected record's EMIS Web patient identifier is used to confirm the match. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Once a match is confirmed in the Unmatched tab, the document moves to the **Patient Matched Documents** tab. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Unmatched documents are not moved to the Patient Matched Documents tab until a patient match has been confirmed. *(Must Have — clinical safety)*
- [ ] The Unmatched tab displays a count badge showing the current number of unmatched documents. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] All manual match resolutions from the Unmatched tab are audited, recording: `user_ern`, `document_ern`, `matched_patient_ern`, `match_source: manual`, `timestamp`. *(Must Have)*

**Acceptance Criteria — Inbox Tab Structure (Increment 1):**
- [ ] The inbox has **two tabs: Unmatched Documents** (default, leftmost) and **Patient Matched Documents**. No other tabs are present in Increment 1. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] All inbound documents land directly in the Documents Manager inbox; there is no preceding Inbox step. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] **The toolbar (search field, filter, sort, and upload controls) is positioned above the tab strip** and remains visible and functional regardless of which tab is active. The toolbar applies its search and filter context to the currently active tab's document list. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] When the user clicks a document in the queue-peek sidebar while in gallery view, the current document's in-progress workflow state is autosaved before the selected document loads; the previously active document retains a visible in-progress badge in the peek list. *(V1d — queue-peek navigation)*
- [ ] Once a patient match is confirmed in the workflow panel, the matched patient's name, date of birth, and NHS number are displayed in a persistent patient banner above the step indicator and remain visible for that document. *(V1d — patient banner; Increment 1: displayed after Match step only)*
- [ ] Completed workflow steps in the step indicator are clickable; clicking a completed step navigates the user back to that step's content without losing data entered in later steps. *(V1d — StepIndicator navigation)*

